-- title:
--   A test function vanishing on K detects distance from the supported subspace
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a pointed metric space with Lipschitz-free space $\mathcal F(M)$; $\mathcal F_K(M)$ is the closed span of $\{\delta_x:x\in K\}$, and $\langle\mu,f\rangle$ is the pairing with a Lipschitz $f$ normalized by $f(0)=0$. An element $\mu$ is $\varepsilon$-approximable on $K$ if $\|\mu-\nu\|<\varepsilon$ for some $\nu\in\mathcal F_K(M)$. If $\|\mu-\nu\|\ge\varepsilon$ for every $\nu\in\mathcal F_K(M)$, then there is a $1$-Lipschitz $f:M\to\mathbb R$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f=0$ on $K$ such that
--   $$
--   \langle\mu,f\rangle\ge\varepsilon .
--   $$
--
--   This is the Hahn–Banach separation in the free space, expressed through Lipschitz functions.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/LipschitzEquivalence/SupportedDuality.lean#L43-L84

